# Runtime arguments for vllm-mlx serve on macOS (Apple Silicon / MLX).
#
# install.sh reads VLLM_MLX_MODEL and VLLM_MLX_ARGS, expands each into the
# LaunchAgent plist's ProgramArguments (launchd does NOT word-split env vars the
# way systemd's ExecStart does), and installs the result. To change flags later:
# edit this file (or the copy install.sh made), then re-run ./install.sh — it
# rewrites the plist and reloads the agent.
#
# install.sh word-splits $VLLM_MLX_ARGS in bash, so a flag taking JSON needs its
# inner quotes escaped and no spaces inside the object:
#   --default-chat-template-kwargs {\"reasoning_effort\":\"medium\"}
# Bare quotes end the assignment early and the value silently arrives as
# {reasoning_effort:medium}, which the server rejects.
#
# The model is an MLX checkpoint resolved by name from the Hugging Face cache;
# vllm-mlx downloads it on first launch if absent. Use an MLX-native quant from
# mlx-community. On Apple Silicon the fastest 4-bit format is mxfp4 (see
# ../llama.cpp/EVALUATIONS-macos.md); nvfp4 exists for parity with the CUDA
# NVFP4 default but is ~7% slower here.
#
# A repo name loads the text-only mlx_lm path. A filesystem path loads the MLLM
# path instead — which is the ONLY one that routes images. For a vision model,
# point this at a directory (symlinks into the HF cache are enough) and use an
# affine `-4bit` quant: the MLLM path dequantizes the base as affine, so an
# mxfp4 base dies on the first token with
#   [metal::Device] Unable to load kernel affine_dequantize_uint8_t_gs_32_b_4
# Local-path serving needs vllm-mlx >= 0.4.1; 0.4.0 returns fluent nonsense.
VLLM_MLX_MODEL="mlx-community/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-mxfp4"

# host 127.0.0.1 keeps the API local-only. 0.0.0.0 exposes it on the LAN; the
# server has NO auth unless --api-key is set, so firewall the port and only do
# this on a trusted network.
#
# Port 8000 matches the vLLM and ds4 deployments in this repo. ds4-macos also
# defaults to 8000; the two can still co-exist if they bind different addresses
# (ds4 on the LAN IP, this on 127.0.0.1), but they share the memory pool — run
# one at a time, or change --port here to avoid confusion.
#
# --reasoning-parser qwen3 enables per-request thinking control: clients pass
# chat_template_kwargs={"enable_thinking": false} to skip the reasoning preamble
# (bare mlx-lm cannot). Drop it for non-Qwen3 models.
VLLM_MLX_ARGS="--host 127.0.0.1 --port 8000 --reasoning-parser qwen3"

# --- Reasoning models: the defaults will bite you ------------------------------
# Everything below was learned running Qwen3.8-27B (EVALUATIONS-macos.md
# 2026-08-19). Agent clients typically send no sampling parameters and no
# max_tokens, so whatever the server defaults to is what they actually get.
#
# Four limits bound a long reasoning run, and vllm-mlx's defaults for all four
# predate models with a 262144 window:
#
#   --timeout 300           the tightest, and the one nobody looks at: it is
#                           WALL-CLOCK. At ~32 tok/s it 504s any answer past
#                           ~9,750 tokens no matter how high the token caps are.
#   --max-request-tokens    ceiling a client may ask for. Default 32768 caps a
#                           262144-window model at 1/8 of it.
#   --max-tokens            per-response default when the client sends none.
#   thinking token budget   reasoning only; forces the end-think sequence when
#                           exhausted. Keep it well under --max-tokens or
#                           reasoning eats the room the answer needs.
#
# Qwen3.8's chat template defaults reasoning_effort to xhigh, and that default
# is not usable. Measured here: at medium both an open-ended SVG and a hard
# counting problem finish (489 s / 213 s). At xhigh the counting problem still
# finishes (516 s, 9,633 chars of reasoning) but the SVG **did not complete in
# an hour** — it ran out the 3600 s timeout with nothing to show.
#
# So the thinking budget cannot be sized from observation: the observed maximum
# is "never finished". It is a policy cap, not a measurement, and --timeout is
# the real backstop. medium is the setting that makes this model usable, and
# 32768 is far above what medium actually spends (~800 reasoning tokens on the
# counting problem), so it bounds a runaway without ever truncating real work.
#
# Its generation_config supplies no min_p, and Qwen's own tracker reports the
# shipped sampling values falling into infinite reasoning loops
# (QwenLM/Qwen3.6#145).
#
# Use the model's THINKING sampling recipe, not the instruct one. Substituting
# temp 0.7 / top_p 0.8 / presence_penalty 1.5 here measurably cost accuracy —
# 5/6 against 9/9 on the same verifiable set — because it suppresses the
# reasoning rather than keeping it well-behaved. presence_penalty is an
# instruct-mode knob for long chats; it distorts a reasoning chain.
#
#VLLM_MLX_ARGS="--host 127.0.0.1 --port 8000 --reasoning-parser qwen3 \
#  --enable-auto-tool-choice --tool-call-parser qwen3_coder \
#  --default-chat-template-kwargs {\"reasoning_effort\":\"medium\"} \
#  --default-temperature 1.0 --default-top-p 0.95 --default-top-k 20 \
#  --default-min-p 0 --default-thinking-token-budget 32768 \
#  --max-tokens 65536 --max-request-tokens 262144 --timeout 3600"
#
# KV for that window is ~64 KB/token on Qwen3.8-27B (16 of 64 layers do full
# attention, 4 KV heads, head_dim 256) — ~16.8 GB at full 262144, affordable on
# a 128 GB box against 15.8 GB of weights. Check the arithmetic for your model
# before copying the numbers.
#
# --enable-auto-tool-choice REQUIRES --tool-call-parser; it errors out without
# one, and with neither an agent driver gets a clean, silent no-op.

# MTP speculative decoding, if the checkpoint ships MTP tensors:
#   --enable-mtp --mtp-num-draft-tokens 2
# Do not expect the GB10 speedup. vllm-mlx pins the native MLX MTP path to
# draft depth 1 and logs that it ignores --mtp-num-draft-tokens, so the head's
# forward pass costs about what the extra token saves: measured 32.4 vs 32.7
# tok/s on Qwen3.8-27B, against 2.2x for the same head under llama.cpp/CUDA.
#
# Other useful knobs: --kv-cache-quantization [--kv-cache-quantization-bits 8],
# --enable-prefix-cache, --continuous-batching, --api-key <token>.
